Ah, I forgot to mention a thing. After the castle, I had a fully charged
wand of wishing and did not really know what to wish for, as I was quite
well-equipped. (It was only later that I noticed to miss an essential
ingredient for ascension, i.e., an amulet of magical breathing.)

So I decided to go back to the black market and try out another way of
disposing of One-eyed Sam. I did not want to do it myself, because I was
lawful and wanted to keep telepathy. I did have a blessed tin of
floating eye meat but considered that as reserve for the accidental
killing of a peaceful priest on Astral. So I wished for a blessed
figurine of a giant shoggoth, applied it next to Sam and watched. While
Sam did defend himself valiantly, Shoggy hat no problems killing him.
What was hilarious is that neither of Sam's assistants came to help.
They all remained peaceful. Strange that he calls them to help at the
slightest offense from the player character but does not when fighting
for his life against some other monster.

I then gathered about five to six as yet unknown spellbooks from the
shop, so the wish was well-spent. Another wish, for a spellbook of
finger of death, was not, because I never got below 46% failure
probability, so the spell was useless to me.

After the game, I tried a few more figurine wishes in wizard mode to
find out, which pet is most useful in killing Sam. There are not many
choices, because he is level 25, so an Archon, for example, will not
attack him. I checked the following variants: a Solar, a Planetar, a
giant shoggoth, a crystal golem, a steel, a sapphire, and a diamond
golem. The results were somewhat sobering. The Planetar and all of the
golems except for the crystal one, will never hit Sam. Since they cannot
injure him, always being blocked by his armor, he will not even bother
to retaliate, so it is an eternal stalemate. Out of three crystal
golems, one succeeded in hitting Sam and eventually killing him, the two
others never managed to hit him. Even when I made one of the failing two
crystal golems, who had started at level 31, first kill all of Sam's
assistants, which made him move up to level 35, he did not succeed in
hitting Sam. This is strange, as Sam was always the same incarnation (I
restarted the same game over and again), with an AC of -30 and 133 HP.

Obviously, hitting probabilities between monsters fighting each other
must be calculated differently from probabilities of monsters hitting
the player. A Planetar does not have any difficulties hitting a player
at an AC of -35 or even -45. But he cannot hit Sam at -30.

The Solar kills Sam fastest, needing below 10 turns. The giant shoggoth
needs a little longer and is in danger of being killed by Sam (one of my
test cases went down in HP so far he did not continue to attack Sam, I
had to lead him through the shop for some time to kill other critters so
he finally could finish the job). Moreover, the giant shoggoth
thoroughly corrodes Thiefbane. In real game, I would nonetheless prefer
the giant shoggoth approach, as there is a chance that your figurine
will not give you a pet but a hostile monster. If you are in a situation
that you still *need* stuff from the shop, you will not in general stand
a chance against a hostile Solar, with wands of death ineffective (it is
possible to kill them with bullets, but you first have to retreat far
enough, and his first attack may already take you out). With a giant
shoggoth, a wand of digging is sufficient to get rid of it, supposing
you have more than 100 HP (about that many you will lose in the first
round at AC -30 against a giant shoggoth). This raises an interesting
question. If Sam has a wand of digging, will he use it against the
shoggoth? Moreover, if you polymorph into a giant shoggoth yourself,
might he kill you this way (rather, make you return to naked human form
and kill you then)? Also, is it possible for Sam to kill a Solar
(sometimes) via the beheading capability of Thiefbane?

That the shoggoth makes Thiefbane less usable, is not really a problem.
Thiefbane is not a very desirable artifact anyway, even if it is
normally already enchanted to +3 or +4. Without drain resistance (which
normally uses up an amulet slot), it is essentially unusable.

I then tried, also in wizard mode, my old approach with bullets and a
boulder castle around me from a blessed scroll of earth to protect me
against Sam's attacks. Even with a blessed +7 assault rifle and blessed
+7 bullets, I could not hit Sam, as long as my experience level was
below 23 or 26 (depending on the two versions of Sam, having AC -30 and
-33, respectively). When going to XP 30, I hit him with about 2 bullets
in a round of 7. So you can still kill him this way, but it is tedious,
and you need many bullets. Sam is the most difficult monster to hit in
the whole dungeon (with the possible exception of the player...).

No ray attack is effective, because he has a shield of reflection.
Fireball is useless, as he is fire resistant and draining does not work
either. So spells are useless against Sam.

I also meleed him. The minimum requirement is to quaff a potion, better
two, of invulnerability before. This will protect you from being
decapitated (for a few turns), but not from Thiefbane's level draining
attack. So if you are not wielding Excalibur, you also need an amulet of
drain resistance. Moreover, I could not hit him with a +7 artifact
(Excalibur/Mjollnir/the Bat from Hell), if I was not at least level 23
myself. (Mjollnir is not good, as Sam is shock resistant.) Starting with
that level, I did hit him occasionally. At level 30, I hit him each
time. So killing him in melee will not become an option before a stage
of the game where you are not likely to need anything from the shop
anymore...

Otherwise, you can still steal from the shop with the help of a magic
whistle and randomly generated cats or dogs (they appear fairly often).
A few identified valuable gems help to build up credit, because contrary
to gold, your pets can pick up reasonable value by picking up some gems.


